\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pathfinder-note}

\title{Mechanism Guarantees and Funding Limits in Carbon Redispatch}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The pair and the connexion pursued}

Q proposes quadratic payments for welfare-maximizing resource
allocation, claiming budget balance, individual rationality, and
stochastic learning stability.
P combines nodal carbon-intensity forecasting with spatial and temporal
scheduling of mobile storage and distributed data centers.

The proposed connexion was to use Q's incentives to implement P's
coordinated dispatch among strategic resource owners. The investigation
instead established a correction of Q and a conditional procurement
analysis: carbon objectives, continued-service outside options, and
strategic participation in clearing cannot be identified with the
guarantees of a centralized optimizer or a prescribed learning rule.
\ledger{1,5,9,10,31,42}

This account consolidates the accepted results rather than presenting
another mechanism extension. Review 54 rechecked the principal algebra
and bounds but rejected unrestricted continuous-clock perfection.
That claim is restricted below and the correction is recorded in
entry 55. The auction ingredients are largely classical; theoretical
novelty remains uncertified, and the operational evidence is thin.
\ledger{44,52,54,55}

\section{The correction of Q}

\subsection{The advertised strict certificate is infeasible}

\begin{claim}
Q's conditions P1(ii) and P2(i) cannot hold simultaneously.
\end{claim}

For a nonzero resource-price perturbation \(q\), set every agent's
perturbation to \(v_n=(0,q)\). Condition P2(i) requires
\[
\sum_m A_{nm}^{n}=0.
\]
Consequently, Q's displayed matrix satisfies
\[
v^\top\Psi v
=-\sum_n q^\top\left(\sum_m A_{nm}^{n}\right)q=0.
\]
Thus \(v^\top(\Psi+\Psi^\top)v=0\), whereas P1(ii) requires
\[
v^\top(\Psi+\Psi^\top)v
\leq-\upsilon N\|q\|^2<0.
\]
The contradiction is independent of the allocation-price blocks.
It invalidates the simultaneous certificate, including the assertion
that Eq.~(40) satisfies it. It does not establish impossibility of
equilibrium implementation.
See \url{ada/analysis.md}, lines 31--49.
\ledger{2,8,13,23,54}

\subsection{The explicit payment violates participation}

At a feasible, complementary consensus-price profile, direct
simplification of Q's Eq.~(40) gives
\[
\lambda=\alpha p,\qquad
t_n=\beta_N\lambda^\top(x_n-c/N),\qquad
\beta_N=\frac{N^2-N+1}{(N-1)^2}>1.
\]
Its own allocation derivative is only \(\lambda\).
The equilibrium payment can therefore exceed the owner's surplus.

Take
\[
N=3,\quad K=1,\quad \alpha=c=1,\quad
\mathcal X_n=[0,2],
\]
\[
V_1(x)=11x-\frac{x^2}{2},\qquad
V_2(x)=V_3(x)=-\frac{x^2}{2}.
\]
The welfare optimum and an actual Nash equilibrium are
\[
x=(1,0,0),\qquad p=(10,10,10).
\]
Each own negative-utility Hessian is
\[
\begin{pmatrix}1&-1/2\\-1/2&1\end{pmatrix}\succ0.
\]
The allocation derivatives of utility are \((0,-10,-10)\), and
the price derivatives vanish. These conditions prove optimality
including the allocation lower bounds, without invoking Q's certificate.
Nevertheless,
\[
(t_1,t_2,t_3)
=\left(\frac{35}{3},-\frac{35}{6},-\frac{35}{6}\right),
\qquad
U_1=\frac{21}{2}-\frac{35}{3}=-\frac76.
\]
Budget balance holds while individual rationality fails.
See \url{ada/analysis.md}, lines 51--93, and
\url{ada/check_mechanism_001.log}, lines 1--9.
\ledger{3,8,13,54}

An opponents-only correction preserves every unilateral incentive:
\[
\Delta t_n=
\frac{\alpha(\beta_N-1)}{N-1}\bar p_{-n}^{\top}
\left(\bar x_{-n}-\frac{N-1}{N}c\right).
\]
At a complementary consensus equilibrium,
\[
t_n+\Delta t_n=\lambda^\top(x_n-c/N).
\]
Complementarity gives budget balance. If \(0\in\mathcal X_n\),
concavity and welfare stationarity imply
\[
V_n(x_n)\geq\nabla V_n(x_n)^\top x_n
\geq\lambda^\top x_n,
\]
so corrected utility is at least \(\lambda^\top c/N\).
This repairs participation relative to zero consumption, conditional
on the equilibrium characterization; it does not repair the strict LMI
or establish participation relative to continued profitable operation.
See \url{ada/analysis.md}, lines 95--120.
\ledger{4,8,10}

For completeness, the uncapped equilibrium characterization follows
directly. For each resource write
\(r=\sum_nx_n-c\) and \(P=\sum_np_n\). Then
\[
F_{p_n}=\frac{\alpha}{N-1}(Np_n-P-r),\qquad
0\leq p_n\perp F_{p_n}\geq0.
\]
Summation gives \(r\leq0\). If \(r<0\), a positive maximum price
would require \(Np_{\max}-P=r<0\), which is impossible.
If \(r=0\), the nonnegative derivatives sum to zero and force
consensus. Allocation stationarity becomes welfare stationarity.
For \(N\geq3\), own strict concavity supplies sufficiency.
Existence requires a welfare multiplier; unique allocation does not
imply unique price.
See \url{ada/analysis.md}, lines 122--130.
\ledger{8,11,13}

\subsection{A feasible replacement certificate and economic design}

The accepted repair concerns a broader affine-resource problem:
\[
\min_{x_n\in X_n}\sum_nf_n(x_n)
\quad\text{subject to}\quad
\sum_nq_n\leq c,\qquad q_n=A_nx_n+e_n.
\]
Assume compact convex \(X_n\), differentiable
\(\alpha\)-strongly convex costs with Lipschitz gradients, and a
primal-dual solution. Signed contributions are permitted.
Set \(M=\max_n\|A_n\|\), and choose
\[
d=\frac{\gamma+b}{N-1},\qquad
w=\frac{\gamma}{Nb},\qquad a,b,\gamma>0,
\]
\[
\alpha\frac{a\gamma}{b(N-1)}
>\frac{(b+d)^2M^2}{4}.
\tag{1}
\]
With \(P_{-n}=\sum_{m\ne n}p_m\) and
\(Q_{-n}=\sum_{m\ne n}q_m\), use
\[
\begin{aligned}
t_n^{\rm core}={}&
\frac a2\|p_n\|^2-\frac a{N-1}p_n^\top P_{-n}\\
&-bp_n^\top(q_n+Q_{-n}-c)
+dP_{-n}^\top q_n.
\end{aligned}
\]
For public entitlements \(h_n\), independent of the owner's report,
add
\[
\begin{aligned}
r_n={}&\frac a{2(N-1)}\sum_{m\ne n}\|p_m\|^2\\
&+\frac b{N-1}P_{-n}^\top(Q_{-n}-c)
-\frac\gamma{N-1}P_{-n}^\top h_n.
\end{aligned}
\]
The own operator is
\[
\begin{aligned}
F_{x_n}&=\nabla f_n(x_n)+A_n^\top(-bp_n+dP_{-n}),\\
F_{p_n}&=\frac a{N-1}(Np_n-P)
-b\left(\sum_mq_m-c\right).
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]
The price argument above gives feasibility, consensus and
complementarity. Condition (1) implies own strict convexity.
Thus equilibria implement the unique welfare allocation with
\(\lambda=\gamma p\), and primal-dual solutions supply equilibria.
Prices may remain nonunique.
\ledger{15,17,23}

At consensus the total tax reduces to
\[
t_n^*=\lambda^\top(q_n^*-h_n).
\]
For local baseline \(x_n^0\), let \(q_n^0=A_nx_n^0+e_n\).
Convexity and stationarity give
\[
f_n(x_n^0)-f_n(x_n^*)-t_n^*
\geq\lambda^\top(h_n-q_n^0),
\]
\[
\sum_nt_n^*
=\lambda^\top\left(c-\sum_nh_n\right).
\tag{3}
\]
Hence \(h_n\geq q_n^0\) guarantees baseline participation.
If the baseline is globally feasible, entitlements can also sum to
\(c\), yielding budget balance. A baseline violating a new carbon
target need not allow both conditions.
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 7--105.
\ledger{17,23}

Q's printed P2--P4 are themselves compatible. In its original
resource setting, replace \(r_n\) by
\[
\begin{aligned}
r_n^{\rm diag}={}&
\frac a{2(N-1)}\sum_{m\ne n}\|p_m\|^2
-\frac b{N-1}\sum_{m\ne n}p_m^\top x_m\\
&-\frac\gamma{N(N-1)}P_{-n}^\top c.
\end{aligned}
\]
P2 holds with \(\theta^n=b\mathbf1\) and
\(\zeta^n=\gamma\mathbf1\); P3 follows from the diagonal sums.
P4 holds when \(\gamma\geq Nb\), because its quadratic sum vanishes,
foreign-demand coefficients are nonpositive, and
\[
\sum_ma_m^n=(b-\gamma/N)c\leq0.
\]
Increasing \(a\) then ensures (1) for every \(N\geq2\).
The equilibrium tax is
\[
t_n^*=
\left(1+\frac{Nb}{\gamma(N-1)}\right)
\lambda^\top(x_n^*-c/N).
\]
Q instead chooses \(a=\alpha\), \(b=\alpha/(N-1)\),
\(\gamma=\alpha\), so its linear sum is positive for nonzero \(c\);
Eq.~(40) fails P4(ii).
The entitlement repair belongs to Q's broad quadratic basis but
generally violates its narrower P3(ii) restriction.
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 107--135.
\ledger{15,17,54}

Let \(A=\operatorname{diag}(A_n)\),
\(\Pi=(I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^\top/N)\otimes I_K\), and
\(W=\operatorname{diag}(I,wI)\). The symmetric derivative of \(WF\)
is bounded below by
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\alpha I&-(b+d)A^\top\Pi/2\\
-(b+d)\Pi A/2&waN\Pi/(N-1)
\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{4}
\]
The common-price cross term cancels because \(wNb=\gamma\).
Condition (1) controls allocations and price disagreement, leaving
a common-price nullspace. Common price must remain in the dynamics.

For \(z=W^{1/2}s\) and \(G(z)=W^{1/2}F(W^{-1/2}z)\), put
\[
D_p=\frac{aN}{N-1},\qquad
\ell=\frac{(b+d)M}{2\sqrt w},\qquad
m=\frac{\alpha+D_p-\sqrt{(\alpha-D_p)^2+4\ell^2}}2>0.
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle G(z)-G(z'),z-z'\rangle
\geq m\bigl(&\|x-x'\|^2\\
&+\|\Pi(z_p-z_p')\|^2\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}
\]
Comparing two variational-inequality solutions proves that a common
componentwise price cap containing one uncapped equilibrium creates
no spurious equilibria: allocations agree, prices are consensual,
and slack resources still have zero price.
This does not identify a sufficient public cap without cost bounds.
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 137--167.
\ledger{17,21,23}

For Q's original rule, the analogous weight is
\(\operatorname{diag}(I,(N-1)I/N)\). Its consensus block is
\(\operatorname{diag}(\alpha,0)\), and each disagreement block is
\[
\alpha
\begin{pmatrix}
1&-\dfrac{2N-1}{2(N-1)^2}\\
-\dfrac{2N-1}{2(N-1)^2}&1
\end{pmatrix},
\]
positive definite for \(N\geq3\).
This surviving monotonicity does not validate Q's printed
VS-PBR representation or Euclidean nonexpansiveness assertion.
See \url{ada/analysis.md}, lines 132--162.
\ledger{7,8,17}

\subsection{Conditional stochastic clearing}

The repaired operator admits a standard two-batch extragradient bound:
\[
\begin{aligned}
y_t&=\operatorname{proj}_Z
\bigl(z_t-h[G(z_t)+\epsilon_t]\bigr),\\
z_{t+1}&=\operatorname{proj}_Z
\bigl(z_t-h[G(y_t)+\eta_t]\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]
Assume conditionally unbiased fresh errors, each with conditional
second moment at most \(\sigma^2/B_t\), and
\(h\leq1/(\sqrt8L)\), where \(L\) is a Lipschitz constant of \(G\).
These assumptions are stronger than Q's variance statement alone.
Define
\[
E_T=D_0^2+8h^2\sigma^2\sum_{t<T}B_t^{-1},
\qquad D_0^2=\|z_0-z^*\|^2.
\]
For the average predictor allocation and exact natural residual,
\[
\mathbb E\|\bar x_T-x^*\|^2\leq\frac{E_T}{2hmT},
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac1T\sum_{t<T}\mathbb E\|\mathcal R_h(z_t)\|^2
\leq\frac{4D_0^2}{h^2T}
+\frac{34\sigma^2}{T}\sum_{t<T}B_t^{-1},\\
\mathcal R_h(z)=
\frac{z-\operatorname{proj}_Z(z-hG(z))}{h}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{6}
\]
Projection optimality and Young's inequality give a one-step decrease
with terms
\[
-\tfrac12\|z_t-y_t\|^2
-\tfrac12\|z_{t+1}-y_t\|^2
-2hmD_t^2+\frac{8h^2\sigma^2}{B_t},
\]
in expectation, where \(D_t^2\) is predictor allocation error plus
price-disagreement error. Freshness cancels the second-batch cross
terms. Telescoping yields (6).
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 169--237.
\ledger{17,23,54}

Taking \(B_t=T\) uses \(2T^2\) samples per owner and \(2T\)
aggregate broadcasts, with squared allocation error of order
\(T^{-1}\). Summable inverse batch sizes give asymptotic mean-square
allocation convergence, without unique multiplier convergence.
For an \(L_R\)-Lipschitz certified saving function and a tightened
target \(R+\rho\),
\[
\mathbb P\{\underline R(\bar x_T)<R\}
\leq\frac{L_R^2E_T}{2hmT\rho^2}.
\tag{7}
\]
This is neither exact deadline feasibility nor a finite-time
settlement guarantee. It assumes compliant oracles and updates;
convergence is not a strategic-compliance theorem.
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 239--249.
\ledger{17,21,31}

\section{What the connexion to P requires}

\subsection{Participation and causal carbon accounting}

Let \(C_n(x_n)\) be private operating cost and \(x_n^0\) continued
baseline operation. Baseline participation requires
\[
C_n(x_n)+t_n\leq C_n(x_n^0).
\]
Writing
\[
\Delta C(x)=\sum_n[C_n(x_n)-C_n(x_n^0)],
\]
budget balance requires \(\Delta C(x)\leq0\).
Nonnegative external net funding must satisfy
\[
S\geq[\Delta C(x)]_+.
\tag{8}
\]
For known costs and unrestricted transfers this bound is attainable.
It is a transfer-feasibility statement, not private-information
implementation. Q's zero-consumption outside option is different;
continued operation and private migration costs are explicit
assumptions of the extension, not primitives established by P.
\ledger{5,9,10,17}

P's frozen average nodal carbon intensities do not identify the
causal effect of redispatch. The notes give a counterexample:
region A initially supplies nine zero-carbon units and one coal unit,
with coal marginal; region B supplies eight coal units and two gas
units, with gas marginal. Coal has emission factor \(1\), gas \(2/5\).
For separated or transfer-constrained regions, moving one demand unit
from B to A changes the average-intensity objective by
\[
\frac1{10}-\frac{22}{25}=-\frac{39}{50},
\]
but increases actual emissions by \(1-2/5=3/5\).
This is not a reanalysis of P's network.
See \url{ada/analysis.md}, lines 233--241.
\ledger{6,11,13}

If a valid finite-shift causal response satisfies
\[
|m_j-\widehat m_j|\leq\delta_j,
\]
then net demand change \(d(x)\) has certified saving
\[
\underline R(x)
=-\widehat m^\top d(x)-\sum_j\delta_j|d_j(x)|.
\]
The complete-information funding frontier is
\[
S_{\rm FI}(R)=
\max\left\{0,\
\min_{\substack{x\in\mathcal F\\\underline R(x)\geq R}}
\Delta C(x)\right\},
\tag{9}
\]
with infeasible targets assigned \(+\infty\).
An interval for average NCI does not justify the causal assumption.
P's binary storage routes also fall outside the convex repair.
Its \(33.86\%\) comparison changes spatial dispatch capability between
Cases 2 and 3; it does not isolate one-hour forecast latency.
See \url{inputs/P.txt}, lines 885--935 and 1287--1292.
\ledger{8,9,10,11}

\subsection{Static funding attainment and its information boundary}

For directed scalar shifts \(z_i\in[0,u_i]\), let certified saving
be \(g_iz_i\), \(g_i>0\), and cost
\[
C_i(z_i)=k_iz_i+\frac{h_i}{2}z_i^2,\qquad k_i\geq0.
\]
Let \(V(R)\) minimize total cost subject to
\(\sum_ig_iz_i\geq R\). The target binds.
The repaired game with \(q_i=-g_iz_i\) and zero entitlements pays
\(\lambda g_iz_i\), so uniform funding is \(\lambda R\).
At differentiability points \(\lambda=V'(R)\); generally
\(\lambda\in\partial V(R)\), and its rent is \(V^*(\lambda)\).
\ledger{16,18}

The opponents-only tax
\[
\chi_i=
\frac{\underline h_i}{2g_i^2}
\left(R-\sum_{j\ne i}g_jz_j\right)^2
\]
preserves the game and learning operator. With true curvature
\(h_i\geq\underline h_i\) and upper-capacity multiplier \(\nu_i\),
\[
S-V(R)
=\sum_i\frac{h_i-\underline h_i}{2}z_i^2
+\sum_i\nu_i z_i.
\tag{10}
\]
Known curvature and no positive capacity multiplier give exact
reimbursement and weak baseline participation at the static Nash
equilibrium. With private curvature the first term is bounded by
\(\frac12\sum_i(\overline h_i-\underline h_i)u_i^2\).
Fixed positive supplements give strict participation at arbitrarily
small additional funding, without proving entry or withdrawal
robustness.
\ledger{18,21,23}

This gap is sharp for settlement observing only the same allocation,
price and public bounds. The alternative type
\[
\widetilde h_i=\underline h_i,\qquad
\widetilde k_i=\lambda g_i-\underline h_i z_i
\]
has the same equilibrium messages and cost
\[
\widetilde C_i(z_i)
=\lambda g_i z_i-\frac{\underline h_i}{2}z_i^2.
\]
Participation for that indistinguishable type forces the payment
used in (10). The argument needs a slope domain containing the
alternative type; richer messages, histories or price encodings
are outside it.
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 321--343.
\ledger{18,21}

Two structural restrictions were also established.
First, Q's original payment basis is affine in allocations at fixed
price. With \(g_i=h_i=1\), \(R=2\),
\[
z^*=(v,2-v),\quad
(k_1,k_2)=(4-v,2+v),\quad \lambda=4,
\]
exact reimbursement requires \(4v-v^2/2\) for owner 1.
No fixed-coefficient affine settlement matches this throughout
\(v\in(1/2,3/2)\).

Second, for vector savings \(G_iz_i\), public curvature \(H_i\),
interior optima and binding constraints, a public left inverse of
\(G_i\) reconstructs
\[
z_i=L_i\left(R-\sum_{j\ne i}G_jz_j\right).
\]
Its quadratic rent can then be removed by an opponents-only term.
If a nonzero kernel direction admits an open private-type family,
opponents' messages remain fixed while the required quadratic
extraction varies. Exact opponents-only extraction is impossible
along that family. A full multidimensional DDC schedule generally
lacks the required rank under one carbon target.
See \url{ada/extension.md}, lines 345--375.
\ledger{19,20}

These are complete-information Nash statements, with costs unknown
to the designer but treated as known among owners. They are not
dominant-strategy or Bayesian truthfulness. At an interior equilibrium,
known curvature and observed allocation and price even reveal
\(k_i=\lambda g_i-h_iz_i\); no formal privacy follows.
\ledger{21,23,31}

\section{Strategic clearing changes the funding comparison}

\subsection{Restricted reports and the larger oracle domain}

Normalize saving to \(y_i=g_iz_i\), with cost
\(a_iy_i+b_iy_i^2/2\), target \(\sum_i y_i=R\), and capacity \(v_i\).
Consider terminal payment
\[
\pi_i^\theta=\lambda y_i-\frac{\theta b_i}{2}y_i^2,
\qquad 0\leq\theta\leq1.
\tag{11}
\]
If reports change only the intercept, clearing gives
\(y_i=[(\lambda-\widetilde a_i)/b_i]_0^{v_i}\).
At an interior allocation, full subtraction pays reported cost,
and true profit is \((\widetilde a_i-a_i)y_i\).
Thus static exact reimbursement is not truthful clearing:
a changed oracle changes opponents' terminal messages.
\ledger{23,25,30}

Without binding capacities, on an active set \(A\), define
\[
T_A=\sum_{j\in A}b_j^{-1},\qquad
d_i^\theta=(1+\theta)b_i+(T_A-b_i^{-1})^{-1}.
\]
Regular intercept equilibria satisfy
\[
\lambda_\theta=
\frac{R+\sum_{i\in A}a_i/d_i^\theta}
{\sum_{i\in A}1/d_i^\theta},
\quad
y_i^\theta=\frac{\lambda_\theta-a_i}{d_i^\theta},
\quad
\widetilde a_i^\theta=\lambda_\theta-b_i y_i^\theta.
\]
Inactive owners need \(a_i\geq\lambda_\theta\).
The residual-price payoff is strictly concave across entry thresholds,
giving global best responses in this domain.
Capacities require comparison of stationary points and endpoints
on every activation/saturation segment; the notes provide a finite
test, not a general equilibrium-existence theorem.
\ledger{25,30,33}

For identical owners,
\[
S_\theta
=aR+\frac{bR^2}{N-1}+\frac{\theta bR^2}{2N}.
\tag{12}
\]
Static rent subtraction raises strategic funding in this example.
It is not a universal ranking. With common curvature and all owners
active, writing \(A_2=\sum_i(a_i-\bar a)^2\) and
\(d_1=b(2N-1)/(N-1)\),
\[
S_1-S_0=\frac b2\left(\frac{R^2}{N}-\frac{A_2}{d_1^2}\right).
\]
The notes exhibit a six-owner reversal and inactive/capacity cases.
For interior efficient and report allocations, excess true cost is
\(\frac12\sum_i b_i(y_i^\theta-y_i^*)^2\).
See \url{ada/reporting.md}, lines 68--181.
\ledger{25,30,31}

For two independent \(a_i\sim U[0,1]\), \(b_i=1\), \(R=2\),
and unbounded nonnegative intercept reports, Bayesian equilibria are
\[
\widetilde a_U(a)=\frac76+\frac{2a}{3},
\qquad
\widetilde a_E(a)=\frac94+\frac a2.
\]
Expected funding is \(5\) and \(575/96\), respectively, and true cost
is \(53/27\) and \(63/32\). If reports are instead restricted to
\([0,1]\), both games admit pooling at report \(1\), with funding
\(4\) and \(3\) and expected true cost \(2\).
These are intercept-domain results. Unenforced gradient interfaces
allow curve and curvature deviations, so they do not establish
equilibrium on that larger domain.
See \url{ada/reporting.md}, lines 183--221.
\ledger{26,29,33,35}

\subsection{A full-oracle counterexample and multiplicity}

Allow continuous nonnegative nondecreasing marginal-cost offers,
while keeping the public-curvature payment (11).
Against affine opposing offers with slopes \(s_j>0\), residual price
on an active segment is
\[
\lambda(y)=L_i-\frac{y}{H_{-i}},
\qquad H_{-i}=\sum_{j\ne i}s_j^{-1}.
\]
Writing \(B_i=(1+\theta)b_i\), true payoff is
\[
U_i(y)=(L_i-a_i)y-
\left(H_{-i}^{-1}+\frac{B_i}{2}\right)y^2.
\tag{13}
\]
Without binding upper capacities, residual-price concavity makes
the stationary quantity globally optimal against arbitrary monotone
offer deviations. An affine ex-post family therefore satisfies
\[
s_i=B_i+\left(\sum_{j\ne i}s_j^{-1}\right)^{-1}.
\]
There is no positive finite-slope solution for two owners.
For \(N\geq3\) and common curvature,
\[
s_\theta=(1+\theta)b\frac{N-1}{N-2}.
\tag{14}
\]
This excludes neither two-owner fixed-state equilibria nor nonlinear
or other equilibrium concepts.
\ledger{35,36,37}

For \(N=3\), \(R=3\), \(b_i=1\), \(v_i>3\), and independent
\(a_i\sim U[0,1]\), offers \(a_i+2y\) under uniform payment and
\(a_i+4y\) under subtraction are ex-post equilibria on the full
monotone-oracle domain. All owners remain active. Exact expectations
are
\[
\begin{array}{l|c|c}
\text{Benchmark}&\mathbb ES&\mathbb E C\\ \hline
\text{Known costs}&35/12&35/12\\
\text{Efficient BIC minimum}&17/4&35/12\\
\text{Truthful uniform, not IC}&9/2&35/12\\
\text{Clarke-pivot VCG}&127/24&35/12\\
\text{Uniform oracle equilibrium}&15/2&47/16\\
\text{Subtraction oracle equilibrium}&2303/192&569/192
\end{array}
\tag{15}
\]
Every row saves \(3\). These values follow by substituting
\(y_i=R/N+(\bar a-a_i)/s_\theta\) and using
\(\mathbb E\sum_i(a_i-\bar a)^2=1/6\).
This repairs the earlier action-domain error without claiming
uniqueness or a universal settlement ranking.
\ledger{37,40,54}

At one fixed state with identical costs and \(q=R/N\), symmetric
affine equilibria instead permit
\[
t=a+\left(B-s\frac{N-2}{N-1}\right)q\geq0,
\qquad
\lambda=a+\left(B+\frac{s}{N-1}\right)q.
\]
For two owners, \(t=a+Bq\) works for every \(s>0\), giving
unbounded equilibrium funding. This does not contradict the
ex-post-family restriction.
\ledger{35,36}

The notes extend unbounded funding to every \(N\geq2\), despite
capacities exceeding the entire target. For \(0<e<q\), \(K>0\), use
\[
m(x)=a+Bq+\frac K{R-(N-1)x},
\qquad
0\leq x\leq\frac{R-e}{N-1},
\]
then its tangent extension. These offers are finite, nonnegative,
continuously differentiable and strictly increasing on compact
domains. Residual price gives, for \(e\leq y\leq R\),
\[
U_i(y)=K+\frac{Bq^2}{2}-\frac B2(y-q)^2.
\]
The tangent extension yields the same upper bound below \(e\);
zero quantity gives zero profit. Hence \(q\) is a global best response,
with funding
\[
S_\theta
=aR+\left(1+\frac\theta2\right)\frac{bR^2}{N}+NK.
\tag{16}
\]
Per-report smoothness does not bound this family uniformly.
Common offer restrictions or a price ceiling change the conclusion.
This is equilibrium multiplicity, not nonexistence.
See \url{ada/oracle_frontier.md}, lines 31--175.
\ledger{36,40,54}

Payment (11) is not general pay-as-bid, which integrates the entire
reported curve. For a regular strictly increasing pay-as-bid offer,
raising an interior portion below the allocated endpoint can leave
allocation and endpoint price unchanged while increasing payment.
That argument excludes the stated smooth interior form, not flat,
mixed or tie-dependent equilibria. The funding reversal above
therefore does not contradict uncertain-demand discriminatory-auction
rankings.
\ledger{28,41}

\section{Truthful benchmarks, uncertainty and deadlines}

\subsection{The bounded-type funding frontier}

For independent slopes on finite public supports, let
\(y^*(a)\) be efficient and
\(X_i(a_i)=\mathbb E_{-i}y_i^*(a_i,a_{-i})\).
The Bayesian incentive envelope gives
\[
U_i(a_i)=U_i(\overline a_i)
+\int_{a_i}^{\overline a_i}X_i(s)\,ds.
\]
Interim participation and Fubini imply
\[
\mathbb ES\geq
\mathbb EW(a)+
\sum_i\int_{\underline a_i}^{\overline a_i}F_i(s)X_i(s)\,ds.
\tag{17}
\]
This is attained by endpoint-normalized Groves payments
\[
\pi_i^*(a)=
W(\overline a_i,a_{-i})
-\sum_{j\ne i}C_j(y_j^*(a);a_j).
\tag{18}
\]
They are DSIC on the specified domain and ex-post individually
rational, with zero utility for the top type.
Thus (17) is the efficient BIC, interim-IR minimum, not a minimum
over inefficient mechanisms. It equals \(23/8\) in the two-owner
uniform-type example and \(17/4\) in (15).
It differs from removal-based VCG when the highest type still supplies.
Correlated types require a different analysis; correlation alone
does not justify surplus extraction.
\ledger{26,30,38}

For committed bounded scalar reports, only \(N+1\) clearing calls
are needed for (18). If every feasible approximate clearing has
objective error in \([0,\epsilon]\), pay
\[
\pi_i^{\rm alg}
=W_{\rm alg}(\overline a_i,a_{-i})
-\sum_{j\ne i}C_j(y_j^{\rm alg};a_j)+\epsilon.
\]
A deviation cannot produce true aggregate cost below \(W(a)\);
truthful cost is at most \(W(a)+\epsilon\).
This proves \(\epsilon\)-DSIC and ex-post IR. With actual and
counterfactual errors \(\delta_0,\delta_i\),
\[
S_{\rm alg}-S^*
=\sum_i\delta_i-(N-1)\delta_0+N\epsilon
\in[\epsilon,2N\epsilon].
\tag{19}
\]
Expected-error versions yield expected incentive and participation
bounds, not realized IR.
\ledger{38,40,54}

For public quadratic curvatures and capacities, aggregate-price
bisection on
\[
[0,P],\qquad
P=\max_i(\overline a_i+b_iv_i),\qquad H=\sum_i b_i^{-1},
\]
followed by interpolation of bracket allocations gives exact total
feasibility and objective error at most \(HP^2\,4^{-L}\).
The count is \((N+1)(L+2)\) aggregate supply evaluations and one
aggregate objective evaluation per clearing.
This requires commitment or verifiable execution; secure aggregation
alone does not enforce truthful subsequent computations.

Conditionally, a trusted feasible-output optimizer with allocation
error \(K/T\) and \(M_C\)-Lipschitz objective needs
\(T\geq KM_C^2/\epsilon^2\). The earlier batch schedule then uses
\(2(N+1)T^2\) samples per owner and \(2(N+1)T\) broadcasts,
plus feasibility repair and objective evaluation.
See \url{ada/oracle_frontier.md}, lines 192--277.
\ledger{38,40,42}

\subsection{Pivotality is interface-dependent}

With unbounded intercept reports, a hard target and no outside
supplier, owner \(i\) is pivotal if
\[
r_i=R-\sum_{j\ne i}v_j>0.
\]
At a sufficiently large report, rivals saturate and
\[
U_i^\theta
=(\widetilde a_i-a_i)r_i
+\frac{1-\theta}{2}b_i r_i^2\longrightarrow\infty.
\]
There is no finite pure report equilibrium, even while centralized
dispatch is feasible. Robust coefficients \(g_i=\widehat g_i-\delta_i\)
and a deadline buffer can create this condition before total
capacity is exhausted. Nonpivotality is necessary for avoiding this
obstruction, not sufficient for general equilibrium existence.
\ledger{27,30}

If a uniform shortfall bound is \(K/T\), the Markov buffer
\(\rho_T=\sqrt{K/(\varepsilon T)}\) stays within the nonpivotal margin
\[
M_{\rm cap}=\min_i\sum_{j\ne i}g_iu_i-R
\]
when
\[
T\geq\frac{K}{\varepsilon M_{\rm cap}^2}.
\tag{20}
\]
Here the sum is over \(j\ne i\), so equivalently
\(M_{\rm cap}=\min_i\sum_{j\ne i}g_ju_j-R\).
The latter expression specifies the intended margin.
This is a sufficient compatibility test requiring uniform \(K\),
not a universal minimum deadline or strategic guarantee for
unbounded reports.
\ledger{30,31}

Bounded-type truthful procurement remains finite through pivotality.
For two independent \(a_i\sim U[0,A]\), physical costs
\(a_iz_i+hz_i^2/2\), capacity \(u\), and
\[
D=\frac{R+\rho}{g-\delta}\in[0,2u],
\qquad
w_0=\min\{h\min(D,2u-D),A\},
\]
define
\[
M_1=\frac{Aw_0}{3}-\frac{w_0^3}{3A}
+\frac{w_0^4}{6A^2},
\qquad
M_2=w_0^2-\frac{4w_0^3}{3A}
+\frac{w_0^4}{2A^2}.
\]
Clipping the efficient difference allocation and integrating the
triangular type-difference distribution gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb EW(D)&=\frac{AD}{2}+\frac{hD^2}{4}
-\frac{M_1}{2h}+\frac{M_2}{4h},\\
\mathcal B(D)&=AD+\frac{hD^2}{4}
-\frac{M_1}{h}+\frac{M_2}{4h}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{21}
\]
The second expression is the efficient BIC funding minimum.
When \(h\min(D,2u-D)\geq A\),
\[
\mathcal B(D)=AD+\frac{hD^2}{4}-\frac{A^2}{8h}.
\]
For \(A=h=1,u=2\), it is \(23/8\) at \(D=2\),
\(63/16\) at \(D=5/2\), and \(8\) at full capacity.
It is finite and continuous through pivotality, though a kink can
occur at \(D=u\) when \(hu<A\).
Substituting the chosen deadline buffer gives a policy-dependent
funding curve, not an optimal deadline theorem.
A certified residual backstop of marginal cost \(p_0\) can instead
bound clearing price and owner funding by \(p_0R\), while changing
dispatch. No optimal backstop or elastic-target policy was derived.
See \url{ada/oracle_frontier.md}, lines 279--338.
\ledger{39,40,42,54}

\section{The restricted normalized-clock benchmark}

\subsection{One observed path and the corrected incentive claim}

For costs
\[
C_i(y)=a_iy+\frac{b_i}{2}y^2,\qquad
a_i\in[\underline a_i,A_i],\quad 0\leq y\leq u_i,
\]
procure feasible total \(D\). Public bounds, curvature and capacities
define
\[
S_i(p)=\left[\frac{p-a_i}{b_i}\right]_0^{u_i},
\qquad
\bar S_i(p)=\left[\frac{p-A_i}{b_i}\right]_0^{u_i}.
\]
A fixed descending clock starts at
\(P=\max_i(A_i+b_iu_i)\).
Continuous bids cannot increase with time, must remain between the
public top- and lower-type supplies, and cannot relinquish clinched
quantity; reaching that boundary clears the auction.
Delivery and these action restrictions must be enforceable.
\ledger{45,46,48}

Let \(\lambda\) be actual clearing, and let
\[
\bar S_i(\lambda_i^+)+s_{-i}(\lambda_i^+)=D,
\qquad y_i^+=\bar S_i(\lambda_i^+).
\]
Use the highest clearing price on a plateau. The pointwise floor
implies \(\lambda\leq\lambda_i^+\), so the necessary rival segments
have already been observed. Monotonicity alone does not ensure this:
the notes give an early-stopping counterexample.
Pay
\[
\pi_i=\bar C_i(y_i^+)
+\int_\lambda^{\lambda_i^+}p\,d s_{-i}(p),
\qquad
\bar C_i(y)=A_iy+\frac{b_i}{2}y^2.
\tag{22}
\]
Against sincere rivals, the integral is their cost change, hence
\[
\pi_i=W(A_i,a_{-i})-\sum_{j\ne i}C_j(y_j).
\]
Thus one observed path computes the normalized Groves payment,
including for pivotal owners, without deleting capacity or running
counterfactual auctions.
See \url{ada/clock.md}, lines 7--72.
\ledger{46,48}

Ordinary clinching pays increments of
\(q_i(p)=[D-s_{-i}(p)]_+\) at the current price, including an initial
mandatory tranche at \(P\). Equation (22) subtracts its top-type rent
\[
K_i=A_i^{\rm clk}(y_i^+)-\bar C_i(y_i^+).
\]
Against sincere rival paths, marginal payment is the decreasing
residual inverse-supply price. Increasing true marginal cost selects
the sincere quantity. The supply floor ensures that the top-type
threshold is reached before any permitted stopping, making \(K_i\)
independent of the owner's deviation. This supports sincere bidding
as an ex-post equilibrium from the prescribed initial state.
\ledger{48,54,55}

\begin{objection}
The earlier claim of ex-post perfection at every legal continuous-clock
history prescribed an inadmissible continuation.
\end{objection}

A legal history can leave an owner's current bid above true supply.
Truncating sincere supply by historical upper and clinched lower
bounds then requires an immediate downward jump, whereas the stated
continuous game forbids jumps. Ausubel's discrete formulation permits
drops and does not resolve this mismatch.

The continuation/perfection statement is therefore restricted to
histories where every owner's current bid is at most its true supply,
so the truncated sincere path is continuous and admissible.
At these histories, rival continuations depend on price and fixed
historical bounds; the normalization is either already fixed or is
reached on that continuation before permitted stopping.
Histories with a bid above true supply are not covered.
Allowing downward jumps would require an assignment rule for jumps
crossing the target and a proof; neither is claimed.
This correction overrides the broader wording in
\url{ada/clock.md}, lines 74--82.
\ledger{54,55}

On the sincere path, (22) gives ex-post IR and the efficient Bayesian
funding minimum. Expected funding in (15) is \(17/4\), compared with
ordinary nonpivotal clinching's \(127/24\).
For the two-owner example in (21), normalized funding remains finite
at \(23/8\), \(63/16\), and \(8\).
These are bounded-type normalizations, not a new general auction
principle or dominant strategies against arbitrary responsive rivals.
\ledger{47,48,50,55}

\subsection{Finite-grid and noisy-response guarantees}

Use a descending grid of width at most \(\Delta\), including
\(0,P,A_i,A_i+b_iu_i\). Interpolate bid vectors and settle at the
interpolated exact-total clearing point. Public top-type curves are
then exact between grid points, preserving counterfactual coverage.

Integrating the inverse of sincere interpolated supply defines a
synthetic cost \(\widetilde C_i\). The hinge representation of
quadratic supply gives
\[
\sup_y|\widetilde C_i(y)-C_i(y)|
\leq E_i=\frac{\Delta^2}{4b_i}.
\tag{23}
\]
Each hinge contributes at most \(\Delta^2/8\) of interpolation area;
the inverse-area identity yields the cost bound.
Against sincere rivals, (22) is Groves payment for their synthetic
costs. Replacing the owner's synthetic cost by its true cost changes
each candidate objective by at most \(E_i\), so any admissible
adaptive path gains at most \(2E_i\).
Adding the fixed supplement \(E_i\) gives ex-post IR.
This is approximate ex-post equilibrium from the initial state,
not finite-grid perfection or exact DSIC.
\ledger{49,50,54}

With \(E=\sum_iE_i\), supplemented funding \(S_\Delta\), and exact
normalized funding \(S^*\),
\[
C(y_\Delta;a)-W(a)\leq2E,
\qquad
|S_\Delta-S^*-E|\leq2(N-1)E.
\tag{24}
\]
These follow by comparing actual and top-type synthetic objectives.
Funding can fall below the exact efficient benchmark because
allocation is only approximately efficient.
The simultaneous round count is at most
\[
J=\left\lceil\frac P\Delta\right\rceil+2N+1.
\]
Taking \(\Delta\leq\min_i\sqrt{2b_i\epsilon}\) gives regret at most
\(\epsilon\). One path is not uniformly faster than committed
bisection's logarithmic precision dependence; no wall-clock advantage
was demonstrated.
See \url{ada/clock.md}, lines 88--133.
\ledger{49,52}

For the additional stochastic model, suppose unbiased sub-Gaussian
intercept estimates have variance proxy \(\sigma_i^2\).
Supply error at most \(e_i\) is preserved by clipping to the public
envelope and the running minimum. On a simultaneous accuracy event,
replace (23) by
\[
E_i=Pe_i+\frac{\Delta^2}{4b_i}.
\]
Independent batches of size
\[
B_i\geq
\frac{2\sigma_i^2}{b_i^2e_i^2}
\log\frac{2NJ}{\delta}
\tag{25}
\]
make that event have probability at least \(1-\delta\).
All potential grid batches are indexed in advance, making the
event uniform over adaptive stopping. Counts are at most \(JB_i\)
samples per owner and \(J\) rounds.
Expected regret is bounded by \(2E_i+2Pu_i\delta\);
a further fixed \(Pu_i\delta\) supplement gives expected participation,
not realized IR on every bad sample path.
These oracle-quality assumptions are not supplied by P's data or Q's
variance assumption and do not enforce sampler compliance.
See \url{ada/clock.md}, lines 136--157.
\ledger{49,50,54}

Only prices and aggregate supply need be broadcast, but settlement
and activity enforcement need owner-level bid histories. An interior
sincere bid reveals \(a_i=p-b_iS_i(p)\) to its observer.
There is no formal privacy guarantee, withdrawal right, coalition
guarantee, baseline-integrity result, or extension to arbitrary
multidimensional or binary schedules.
See \url{ada/clock.md}, lines 159--165.
\ledger{50,52}

\section{Evidence, sources and attribution}

The numerical checks supplement the proofs, rather than establish
generality or operational validity. The retained logs cover exact
Q counterexamples and repaired parameters; stochastic clearing trials;
indistinguishable-type funding bounds; intercept best responses with
entry and capacities; 540 full-oracle rational profiles; 42 smooth
high-funding constructions; 60 approximate-Groves cases; and 12
robustness-frontier quadratures.
The clock log covers 36 exact grid profiles, 480 monotone deviations,
the exact funding expectations, pivotal targets, and 100 bounded-noise
activity repairs. See
\url{ada/check_extension_001.log},
\url{ada/check_funding_interface_001.log},
\url{ada/check_reporting_002.log},
\url{ada/check_oracle_frontier_002.log}, and
\url{ada/check_clock_001.log}.
The earlier printed count of 84 smooth constructions was corrected
to 42. No check reproduces P's IEEE-33 application.
\ledger{8,18,21,30,40,50}

Outside sources were obtained or attempted through the shared paper
pool, preferring LaTeX where available. The following comparisons
constrain the contribution.

\begin{itemize}
\item
Lu et al., \emph{A Market-Clearing-based Sensitivity Model for
Locational Marginal and Average Carbon Emission},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.13182}, already distinguish average
attribution and marginal response and are cited by P.
The average-versus-causal objection is not independently novel.
\ledger{6,11}

\item
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08195} already studies joint
electricity-carbon pricing with welfare, budget and participation
guarantees. Its inspected outside option is no trade.
Muthirayan et al.,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.02923}, address baseline reporting;
only the abstract and introduction were audited.
\ledger{6,11}

\item
Wei and Anastasopoulos,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.00952}, already combine Nash
implementation, budget balance, participation and learning.
Hsieh et al.,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10162}, discuss increasing-batch
stochastic extragradient. The repaired learning argument is a
specialization of established projection methods.
\ledger{21}

\item
Chen,
\url{https://yanchen.people.si.umich.edu/published/Chen_ET_2002.pdf},
Healy--Mathevet,
\url{https://doi.org/10.3982/TE898},
and Duggan--Roberts,
\url{https://www.sas.rochester.edu/psc/duggan/papers/pollution3.pdf},
supply precedents for informed-agent Nash mechanisms, stable
price-quantity designs, and distinct complete-information and
Bayesian pollution mechanisms. Inspected results do not justify
zero-rent independent-private-type procurement.
\ledger{28,31}

\item
Vanelli--Como--Fagnani,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13920}, contain the same unit-curvature
activation-price payoff as the full-subtraction intercept game,
distinguish whole-path discriminatory payment, and restrict offer
spaces. The report-game reduction is not a new auction concept.
Johari--Tsitsiklis, Baldick--Grant--Kahn, Holmberg and Genc were
screened through primary records or abstracts; failed full-text
retrievals prevent theorem-level comparisons.
\ledger{28,41}

\item
Ausubel, \emph{An Efficient Ascending-Bid Auction for Multiple Objects},
\url{https://doi.org/10.1257/0002828043052330}, was retrieved and
checked for divisible goods, activity, sincere continuation and
Theorem 1. The pooled text is identified by
\url{sources/other/3229bbf22d505f7c9cb7}.
Ordinary clinching is classical. Its discrete activity formulation
does not prove the rejected all-history continuous claim.
The bounded normalization and grid estimates are derived in the notes.
See \url{ada/clock.md}, lines 167--185.
\ledger{47,51,54,55}

\item
Klemperer--Meyer,
\url{https://doi.org/10.2307/1913707},
Green,
\url{https://doi.org/10.2307/2950646},
Wilson,
\url{https://doi.org/10.2307/1884475},
and Back--Zender,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/6.4.733},
are essential supply-function and divisible-auction predecessors.
Krishna--Perry's \emph{Efficient Mechanism Design},
\url{https://ratio.huji.ac.il/files/dp133.pdf},
and Nisan--Ronen,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1006/game.1999.0790},
respectively anticipate efficient-payment normalization and strategic
manipulation of computation.
These comparisons were limited by failed full-text retrievals.
The title-only Krishna--Perry request returned an unrelated 2025
paper and was explicitly excluded from the audit.
\ledger{51}

\item
Montero,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.98.1.496},
already uses environmental-auction paybacks. Only the publisher
abstract was checked; repeated pool failures blocked the full
comparison. Other attempted classical retrievals, including
Myerson, Cremer--McLean, Moore--Repullo, Kwerel, Varian and Walker,
were not treated as unseen theorem evidence.
\ledger{28,31,41,51}
\end{itemize}

Retrieval limits and searches are preserved in
\url{ada/source_fetch_004.log} through
\url{ada/source_fetch_015.log}.
The exact independent-type funding envelope was derived directly;
neither it nor the supply-function examples establish a new general
mechanism theory. The theorem-level attribution audit remains open.
\ledger{31,41,51,54}

\section{What remains open and the smallest decisive step}

The strongest supported result is Q's infeasible strict certificate
and explicit negative-utility equilibrium, together with a feasible
economic design and a weaker weighted stability certificate.
The broader audit shows that static Nash implementation and compliant
learning convergence do not establish strategic validity of an
interactive procurement service. The restricted normalized clock
provides a constructive benchmark, not an unrestricted-oracle solution.
\ledger{42,52,54}

The continuous-clock guarantee retained here is from-start ex-post
equilibrium. Continuation optimality, or perfection restricted to the
specified histories, requires every current bid to be no greater than
true supply. Histories above true supply remain uncovered, and no
target-crossing jump rule has been proved. Finite-grid guarantees
remain from-start approximate ex-post equilibrium.
The correction is recorded in entry 55; it does not retract the
payment identities, funding comparisons or grid bounds.
\ledger{49,54,55}

The application material is thin. No causal carbon coefficient,
private-cost support or baseline has been calibrated for P's network;
no IEEE-33 reduction or latency advantage has been reproduced.
Authenticated delivery, baseline and entry integrity, formal privacy,
and multidimensional or integer feasibility remain unresolved.
The scalar results cannot be promoted to those guarantees.
\ledger{21,31,42,50,52}

No further abstract extension is needed to settle the identified
overclaim. The correction of Q can proceed to independent paper review
using its displayed formula, the consensus-direction contradiction and
the exact \(U_1=-7/6\) equilibrium. Any stronger novelty claim first
needs the blocked theorem-level comparison with classical
supply-function, efficient-payment and clinching results.
\ledger{54,55}

For the connexion to P, the smallest decisive operational test remains
a baseline-controlled scalar DDC shift with fixed storage routes.
Validate the finite-shift causal coefficient, capacities, type bounds
and incremental costs; then compare committed clearing and normalized
clock procurement at equal service, certified saving and deadline.
Measure paired actual generation emissions, real cost, funding,
owner utility and delivery feasibility.
That test would establish whether the proven interface distinctions
matter in P's setting. Its absence leaves a focused correction and
conditional theoretical audit, rather than a validated carbon
procurement application.
\ledger{9,10,31,42,49,52,54}

\end{document}
